  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nAn upper level trough continues to drift northeastward across \nthe Upper Midwest. A shortwave in southwest flow ahead of the \nlow, and a surface cold front, will complete their passage \nacross the Upper Ohio Valley region this morning. Rain \nassociated with this system should taper off from west to east \nearly this morning after the passage of the front. \n\nPartial clearing is then expected by this afternoon as low level\nridging briefly builds across the region. Clouds will increase\nagain tonight into Friday as a deepening upper low develops in\nthe trough, and tracks southeast across the Great Lakes region.\nThis low is expected to result in periodic rain across the\nregion Friday and Saturday. The latest operational and ensemble\nguidance are in good agreement that the upper low should be\ncentered across wrn PA Saturday morning. The 850 mb temperatures\nunder the low drop to -1 to -3 deg C, which could result in some\nsnow mixing in with the rain across the higher elevations of\nTucker county WV on Saturday, though no accumulation is\nexpected. Otherwise boundary layer temperatures should be warm\nenough for rain elsewhere. High temperatures should be much\ncooler on Saturday, with readings around 5 degrees below\naverage.\n\nThe upper low should exit the region Saturday night, with\ndiminishing rain chances. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nModel ensembles indicate a broad upper trough should persist\nacross the NE CONUS through the middle of the upcoming week, as\nsurface high pressure builds across the area. This pattern\nshould result in mainly dry and seasonably cool temperatures\nacross the region from Sunday into the middle of next week.",
